  "query_preview": "..\nKey Message 1)\nSurface high pressure will remain centered over the ILN fa today.\nWhile some drier air is still hanging around, dewpoints have ticked\nup slightly higher compared to the previous days. Given this trend, \ncombined with the mostly clear skies/calm winds with the surface \nhigh, some patchy dense fog may develop tonight, even outside of the \nriver valleys. Areas that will be most prone include portions of \ncentral and southeast Ohio into northern Kentucky for overland fog. \nWill continue to monitor obs this morning to determine if any \nproducts are needed for the fog.\n\nTemperatures will begin to trend warmer today as return flow develops\nacross the Ohio Valley. Some locations near the Ohio River could hit\nthe 80 degree mark today. This will denote the beginning of a \nwarming trend that continues into Thursday, with Thursday likely \nbeing the warmest day of the week. This is a result of the H5 ridge \nbeing slightly more amplified, which is ushering in warmer air but \nalso keeping the stream of moisture and associated precip just NW of \nour CWA. The ridge dampens on Friday and is replaced by a trough \nejecting through the region, resulting in likely PoPs for our entire \nfa. However, thunder chances appear to be more limited with this \nround of precip.\n\nRain chances will continue on Saturday and perhaps even into Sunday,\nbut will continue to see decreasing chances from north to south as \nwe progress through the weekend.",
